\[\left(\frac{dP_{S_{d}}}{dP_{\beta}}\right)_{t}=\exp\left(\int_{0}^{t}-\tfrac {1}{2}\|\sigma_{d}(u)\|^{2}\,du+\int_{0}^{t}\sigma_{d}(u)^{\top}\,dW(u)\right).\]

Through the Girsanov Theorem (see Appendix B), we find that changing measure from \(P_{\beta}\) to \(P_{S_{d}}\) has the effect of adding a drift to \(W\). More precisely, the process \(W^{d}\) defined by

\[dW^{d}(t)=-\sigma_{d}(t)\,dt+dW(t) \tag{1.48}\]

is a standard Brownian motion under \(P_{S_{d}}\). Making this substitution in (1.41), we find that

\[\frac{dS_{i}(t)}{S_{i}(t)} = r(t)\,dt+\sigma_{i}(t)^{\top}\,dW(t) \tag{1.49}\] \[= r(t)\,dt+\sigma_{i}(t)^{\top}\,[dW^{d}(t)+\sigma_{d}(t)\,dt]\] \[= [r(t)+\sigma_{i}(t)^{\top}\sigma_{d}(t)]\,dt+\sigma_{i}(t)^{\top }\,dW^{d}(t)\] \[= [r(t)+\Sigma_{id}(t)]\,dt+\sigma_{i}(t)^{\top}\,dW^{d}(t)\]

